  of the virus it has the most cases of  the variance compared to other provinces  but those variants are taking hold in  much of western canada  nearly all of saskatchewan's new coven  19 cases  are from the b-117 variant and they're  concentrated  in regina as roberta bell reports there  are calls for more restrictions  certainly the fact that where the hot  spot in the country does make me  concerned  a year ago today everyone was thinking  it was going to be over in two weeks  in the early days of the pandemic there  were proportionately very few  coveted cases in saskatchewan's capital  for a period of weeks last spring  there were actually none at all but now  public health officials say they're  watching regina closely  two weeks three weeks ago we couldn't  predict where  the veins of concern would take over  right now they've taken over in china  there are 527 active coveted cases in  regina  489 of them 93 percent are confirmed or  presumptive variants of concern  this epidemiologist warns it won't stay  local for long  we are seeing a surge in regina  but it will soon be seen in other  communities in saskatchewan as well  in fact as of thursday saskatchewan  reported a total 568 confirmed or  presumptive variant of concern cases  making up 44 percent of the province's  1282  active cases the nature of this pandemic  is now pivoting to a more threatening  more dangerous face regina mayor sandra  masters says she's watching the  situation closely  my offer was we're prepared to do  anything that the chief medical officer  is is thinking will be necessary  restrictions in saskatchewan  relaxed last week to allow expanded  household bubbles of up to 10 people  regardless of what you're allowed to do  just because you can doesn't mean you  should  while regina residents aren't facing any  new public health orders  provincial officials say vaccines are  being diverted to them in a race against  the rapidly spreading variants  roberta bell global news regina  still ahead new rules for medically  assisted death  why advocates for the most vulnerable  are worried  there's new hope tonight for canadians  suffering with intolerable pain  a bill expanding access to assisted  death has received royal assent  those who aren't near their natural  death now have the right to seek medical  assistance in dying  but as heather york's west reports some  groups feel the new law puts  some vulnerable canadians at risk janet  hopkins has been living with a serious  autoimmune disease for the last 13  years she says she lives in constant  pain  since last february i have been  thinking of suicide i've talked to my  family about it  because the pain is excruciating when  you get a flare-up  because her condition was not  life-threatening hopkins had not been  eligible for a medically assisted death  but with the passing of bill c-7 on  wednesday  that all changed i started to cry  because i've wanted this for so long  under the new law intolerably suffering  canadians who aren't near death become  immediately eligible it also removes a  requirement that patients be able to  give  final consent at the time of their death  and that's wonderful because what's  happened in the past is many people have  had to opt to take their death early in  order to avoid that situation because  they  were at such fear and risk of losing  capacity the government had until next  week to bring the law into compliance  with the 2019 quebec superior court  ruling  that found canada's previous law was  unconstitutional  in two years access will also expand to  those suffering with severe  mental illness something opponents worry  will put vulnerable canadians  at risk particularly those from  indigenous communities  where mental health supports can be  difficult to access  it sends a culturally and socially  acceptable message that suicide  is one option to  end one's suffering and that is contrary  to our message  that we're sending to our youth the new  law requires people not facing imminent  death  undergo a 90-day assessment before a  medically assisted death  can be accessed heather urex west global  news calgary  up next atlantic bubble 2.0 and why  there's a burst  of hope  the exclusive atlantic travel bubble is  making a comeback  round two is expected to begin in april  once again allowing locals only to move  freely  while restricting outsiders from  visiting a spike in kovan 19 cases in  november bursts the bubble but as ross  lord reports  with vaccinations on the rise there's  renewed hope  in this part of canada for atlanta  canada re-establishing the atlantic  bubble is a show of confidence i think  that everyone has been wanting to see  opportunities to travel throughout our  region the bubble imploded in november  after a spike in covert 19 cases forcing  people to self-isolate  if they traveled outside of their own  province two weeks there and then to  come back and isolate for another two  weeks and  i it's just too hard it's it's difficult  to take that amount of time  off to make a trip to new brunswick  it's been tough on kathleen armstrong  and tough on her mother  grace no matter if they're 5 years old  or 30 years old  they need their mom and dad they they  like to share their news  especially this news my fiance brad and  i got engaged the first weekend in  december they're itching to celebrate  the ring in person  it's an exciting time i i'm waiting to  actually see it and feel it and touch it  and  and all that travel restrictions will  ease on april 19th  talks are also underway about allowing  visitors to enter from outside the  atlantic region  i'm pretty confident that you know when  we put this behind us i think all of  atlanta canada  is going to jump up a notch or two in  everybody's list excitement  is already building it was instantly a  phone call to mom like  they're saying this date and we've got  to get on the road and i'm calling  boutiques and you need to look at hotels  and  tell my sister to take time off of work  so she can come too  new hope that a return to normal is  getting closer  ross lord global news  and that is global national for this  thursday night i'm robin gill  we'll stay in the atlantic for tonight's  your canada which is chelten beach in  prince edward island  we would love to see your corner of the  country so please keep sending your  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

